WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:03.020
Sometimes, everything goes right.

00:00:03.020 --> 00:00:06.779
Sometimes, all the pieces are in the perfect
position.

00:00:06.779 --> 00:00:09.080
Your teammates.

00:00:09.080 --> 00:00:11.210
Your opponents.

00:00:11.210 --> 00:00:14.500
All of them are arranged, just right.

00:00:14.500 --> 00:00:20.760
Sometimes, all the decisions you make, lead
up to one perfect moment.

00:00:20.760 --> 00:00:26.260
Sometimes, when the moment is just right, 
you can show the world what it takes to win

00:00:26.270 --> 00:00:30.800
six million dollars, through skill and trust
alone.

00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:34.460
[Caster audio]

00:00:35.140 --> 00:00:40.100
Evil Geniuses were considered one of the strongest
Dota 2 teams in the world, heading into

00:00:40.100 --> 00:00:42.160
The International 5.

00:00:42.160 --> 00:00:45.440
Despite kicking off the year by losing two
of their strongest players,

00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:47.440
Artour “Arteezy” Babaev

00:00:47.440 --> 00:00:49.180
and Ludwig “zai” Wahlberg,

00:00:49.180 --> 00:00:53.840
ppd: Nobody wanted to play with us, everyone thought that we were on the losing end of this Western shuffle

00:00:53.840 --> 00:00:58.300
EG still managed to win the Dota 2 Asia Championships
with the help of

00:00:58.300 --> 00:01:00.280
Kurtis “Aui_2000” Ling

00:01:00.280 --> 00:01:03.020
and a young, unproven pubstar named

00:01:03.020 --> 00:01:05.079
Syed “SumaiL” Hassan.

00:01:05.079 --> 00:01:10.360
EG went on to place second at The Summit 3
and ESL One Frankfurt, putting them on track

00:01:10.360 --> 00:01:13.180
to be the one of the favorites heading into
TI5.

00:01:13.180 --> 00:01:19.490
EG finished first in their group, and moved
on to the bracket stage, when they beat compLexity

00:01:19.490 --> 00:01:21.799
and EHOME, qualifying for the upper bracket
finals.

00:01:21.800 --> 00:01:26.940
But it was there that EG faced their first
true challenge all tournament.

00:01:28.200 --> 00:01:32.020
CDEC Gaming went undefeated in the wild card
stage,

00:01:32.020 --> 00:01:40.160
[Caster audio]

00:01:40.160 --> 00:01:44.540
and placed second in their group — second
only to EG.

00:01:44.540 --> 00:01:50.280
But CDEC beat both Cloud9 and LGD-Gaming 2-0
to make it to the Winners’ Finals,

00:01:50.280 --> 00:01:53.060
where they trounced EG 2-0.

00:01:53.060 --> 00:01:55.360
ppd: We kind of thought we knew how to play against  them

00:01:55.400 --> 00:01:59.000
we kind of thought we could handle their playstyle but we were totally wrong.

00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:04.480
Fear: Everything just started going wrong for us. Like, we overestimated, like our own abilities.

00:02:04.480 --> 00:02:08.320
And we underestimated them, and they just completely destroyed us in that best-of-two.

00:02:08.440 --> 00:02:09.880
It was a very fast 2-0.

00:02:12.380 --> 00:02:15.760
EG dropped down to the lower bracket and
beat LGD 2-0

00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:18.520
to earn another shot at CDEC.

00:02:18.520 --> 00:02:24.240
EG took Game 1, but CDEC fought back, taking
Game 2 and keeping it close.

00:02:24.240 --> 00:02:39.160
[Caster audio]

00:02:39.160 --> 00:02:44.720
EG rallied in Game 3, but Game 4 was where
EG made magic happen.

00:02:44.720 --> 00:02:47.060
Aui_2000: Going back in to face CDEC in the Grand Finals,

00:02:47.060 --> 00:02:51.220
it was really useful that we lost to them in the Winners' Finals.

00:02:51.220 --> 00:02:55.740
Just because we were able to sort of predict what they wanted to do in the games.

00:02:55.740 --> 00:02:58.680
They wanted to do like, invade our jungle. We knew what they would ban, we knew

00:02:58.680 --> 00:03:02.780
that they weren't strong with Leshrac. We had a lot of key points of information that we could use.

00:03:03.800 --> 00:03:09.120
Throughout the series, EG targeted Agressif,
forcing him to play his weaker heroes.

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:12.260
It put EG at an advantage through most of
the series.

00:03:12.260 --> 00:03:17.640
[Caster audio]

00:03:17.640 --> 00:03:22.340
ppd: I think this threw them off a lot because they expected us to first pick Naga Siren.

00:03:22.340 --> 00:03:24.340
Because that's one of our best heroes.

00:03:24.780 --> 00:03:27.820
And they thought they were going to get Gyrocopter for Agressif.

00:03:28.720 --> 00:03:32.300
But we knew that they didn't play Naga, we had no reason to prioritize it.

00:03:32.900 --> 00:03:36.740
When Gyropcopter is just like such a deny pick from them.

00:03:36.960 --> 00:03:40.940
CDEC was forced to let SumaiL back on his signature
Storm Spirit.

00:03:40.940 --> 00:03:46.800
[Caster audio]

00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:50.520
And to prevent him from wreaking havoc, they
tried to focus him.

00:03:50.520 --> 00:04:15.660
[Caster audio]

00:04:15.660 --> 00:04:20.840
With SumaiL down, CDEC was desperate, so they
needed to move fast.

00:04:20.840 --> 00:04:26.200
They rushed for Roshan, hoping they could
win the ensuing fight 4v5.

00:04:26.200 --> 00:04:52.900
[Caster audio]

00:04:52.900 --> 00:04:59.240
With CDEC so grouped up, ppd and Universe’s
combo utterly shredded their health bars.

00:04:59.240 --> 00:05:06.980
[Russian caster audio]

00:05:06.980 --> 00:05:10.000
Effectively winning the game then and there.

00:05:10.000 --> 00:05:18.740
[Korean caster audio]

00:05:18.740 --> 00:05:25.700
EG couldn’t see all of CDEC in the pit,
in fact, Universe was effectively going in blind.

00:05:25.700 --> 00:05:28.020
But he trusted his intuition.

00:05:28.020 --> 00:05:29.380
He trusted his team.

00:05:29.380 --> 00:05:32.620
Universe: We didn't have vision of them at the time. But then I told ppd to throw

00:05:32.640 --> 00:05:33.780
in an Ice Vortex.

00:05:33.780 --> 00:05:37.220
And he threw it in there and I saw a couple of them so I just assumed they'd all be in there

00:05:37.220 --> 00:05:39.160
and I just made a blind leap of faith.

00:05:39.160 --> 00:05:41.240
And then they were all in there, and they all died.

00:05:41.600 --> 00:05:45.080
An Echo Slam hits every enemy around the Earthshaker,

00:05:45.080 --> 00:05:48.580
which means teams tend to avoid grouping up against him.

00:05:48.580 --> 00:05:53.460
But either out of desperation or confidence,
CDEC were in the perfect position for

00:05:53.460 --> 00:05:56.580
the biggest Echo Slam in Dota history.

00:05:56.580 --> 00:06:00.800
[Caster audio]

00:06:00.800 --> 00:06:03.700
Universe: I just yelled. I don't even know what sound came out of my mouth,

00:06:03.700 --> 00:06:05.880
but it was pretty loud.

00:06:07.620 --> 00:06:12.120
It wasn’t just a huge play, it was a championship-winning
play.

00:06:12.120 --> 00:06:18.620
It single-handedly won EG the tournament and
finally gave NA Dota some recognition.

00:06:18.620 --> 00:06:24.310
EG hasn’t quite been the same team since
they won TI, but the Echo Slam lives on as

00:06:24.310 --> 00:06:27.260
one of the biggest plays in Dota 2 history.

00:06:27.260 --> 00:06:33.200
It lives on as a reminder that trust and teamwork
can always win a championship.

